{
  "evidence": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "fact": "In Pruitt v. Mote, 503 F.3d 647 (7th Cir. 2007), the Seventh Circuit established a two-part test for appointing counsel in civil cases, requiring courts to consider the litigant's efforts to obtain counsel and their competence to litigate.",
      "source_date": "2007-07-12"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "fact": "In Walker v. Price, 900 F.3d 933 (7th Cir. 2018), the Seventh Circuit held that district courts abuse their discretion when they fail to consider the complexities of advanced-stage litigation and the litigant's ability to handle them, particularly when a case moves to trial.",
      "source_date": "2018-08-20"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "fact": "In James v. Eli, 889 F.3d 320 (7th Cir. 2018), the Seventh Circuit reversed a summary judgment and vacated a denial of counsel, finding that the district court abused its discretion by failing to consider case complexity, the prisoner's distance from witnesses, and the difficulty of medical litigation.",
      "source_date": "2018-05-02"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "fact": "Under 28 U.S.C. § 1915(e)(1), federal courts have the discretion to request an attorney to represent any person unable to afford counsel in civil cases, though there is no constitutional or statutory right to appointed counsel.",
      "source_date": "1996-01-01"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "fact": "In James Pennewell v. James Parish, 923 F.3d 486 (7th Cir. 2019), the Seventh Circuit reversed the district court's denial of counsel, ruling that the district court abused its discretion by not giving particularized consideration to the case complexity and Pennewell's limitations as a legally blind prisoner.",
